# (placement, category, question, answer)
FAQS = [
    ('home', None, 'When should I speak with a town planner?', 'Early in the process is helpful, especially before you commit to a site, finalise a design or lodge an application. An initial discussion can help identify the questions worth investigating first.'),
    ('home', None, 'Can you help me understand whether my idea is feasible?', 'Permits Hub offers planning and feasibility guidance. Share the property address and a short description of your idea so the team can discuss the appropriate next step.'),
    ('home', None, 'Do all projects follow the same approval process?', 'No. The pathway depends on the property, proposed use, design and applicable planning controls. The site can help you start the conversation, but project-specific advice comes after a review of your details.'),
    ('home', None, 'What is the difference between a planning permit and a building permit?', 'A planning permit considers whether a use or development is appropriate for the land under the planning scheme, and is issued by council. A building permit deals with construction standards and safety, and is issued by a building surveyor. Many projects need both, usually planning first.'),
    ('home', None, 'What are zones and overlays?', 'Zones set out which uses are allowed on land and the general rules for development. Overlays add extra controls for specific issues such as heritage, flooding, bushfire or vegetation. Together they shape what needs a permit.'),
    ('home', None, 'What should I include in my first enquiry?', 'A property address or general location, the type of development, your current stage and any drawings or plans you already have are a useful starting point.'),
    ('faqs', 'Getting started', 'When should I seek town planning advice?', 'As early as possible, and ideally before you commit to purchasing a property, signing a commercial lease, or finalizing architectural drawings. Planning controls shape what can be built, what uses are permitted, and what parking and traffic requirements will apply.'),
    ('faqs', 'Getting started', 'What information should I have ready for a first consultation?', 'Any details you have are helpful: the property address or Certificate of Title, concept sketches, existing site photos, and a brief description of what you intend to do (for example, desired child places for a childcare facility or floor area for commercial use).'),
    ('faqs', 'Getting started', 'How do I know if my project needs a planning permit in Victoria?', 'It depends on the site zoning, planning overlays (such as Heritage, Design & Development, or Special Building Overlays), and the specific land use or building works proposed. We conduct a preliminary planning review to establish exactly which permit triggers apply.'),
    ('faqs', 'Getting started', 'How do I check the zoning and overlays on a property?', 'The Victorian Government’s VicPlan tool shows the zone and overlays that apply to a property address. A town planner can explain what those controls mean for your proposal and which permits are likely to be needed.'),
    ('faqs', 'Getting started', 'What is site due diligence before buying land?', 'Site due diligence is a review of the planning controls, constraints and development potential of a property before you commit to buying or developing it. It helps you understand approval risks, likely permits and a practical pathway early, when changes are least costly.'),
    ('faqs', 'Planning and building permits', 'How do I get a planning permit in Victoria?', 'You apply to the local council, which is the responsible authority under the planning scheme. The usual steps are: check the property’s zone and overlays and whether a permit is needed; prepare the application with plans and supporting documents, often including a town planning report; lodge it with council; respond to any request for further information; and, where required, notify neighbours before council decides. Permits Hub can prepare and manage the application for you.'),
    ('faqs', 'Planning and building permits', 'What is the difference between a planning permit and a building permit?', 'A planning permit is granted by the local council and decides how land can be used and developed in relation to neighbourhood character and amenity. A building permit is issued by a private or municipal building surveyor and ensures construction adheres to the Building Code of Australia and Victorian building regulations.'),
    ('faqs', 'Planning and building permits', 'How long does a council planning permit application usually take?', 'Standard statutory timelines are nominally 60 statutory days, but overall timeframes typically range from 3 to 9 months depending on council requests for further information (RFIs), public advertising, and referrals to external authorities such as VicRoads or water authorities.'),
    ('faqs', 'Planning and building permits', 'What happens when council requests further information (RFI)?', 'Council commonly issues an RFI letter outlining specific technical clarifications needed—such as traffic swept paths, acoustic reports, or drainage layouts. Permits Hub coordinates with your consultants to ensure all requested items are addressed comprehensively in a single submission.'),
    ('faqs', 'Planning and building permits', 'Do I need a planning permit to subdivide land?', 'Subdividing land in Victoria generally needs a planning permit, followed by later certification steps, although the specific requirements depend on the zone, overlays and the type of subdivision. A due diligence review can confirm what applies to your property.'),
    ('faqs', 'Planning and building permits', 'Do I need a planning permit for a childcare centre?', 'In most zones a childcare centre needs a planning permit for the use and development, and council will typically look at matters such as traffic, parking, amenity and neighbouring uses. Permits Hub has shared approvals for childcare centres in Winchelsea and St Leonards.'),
    ('faqs', 'After an approval', 'What happens after council approves the planning permit?', 'A permit is issued with conditions (Condition 1 commonly requires amended plans before endorsement). Permits Hub assists in reviewing and coordinating the satisfaction of these conditions so your plans can be officially endorsed for construction.'),
    ('faqs', 'After an approval', 'How do we transition from planning approval to a building permit?', 'Once planning plans are endorsed, detailed engineering and architectural working drawings must be submitted to a building surveyor. Having a coordinator ensures that the building documentation faithfully mirrors the approved planning conditions without conflict.'),
    ('faqs', 'Working with Permits Hub', 'What does a town planner do?', 'A town planner assesses how planning controls apply to a site, advises on what is likely to be approved, prepares the planning application and reports, and deals with council during assessment. Permits Hub also supports building permit documentation and project management through construction.'),
    ('faqs', 'Working with Permits Hub', 'How much does a town planner cost?', 'Fees depend on the size and complexity of the project and the services you need, such as due diligence, a permit application or ongoing management. Council application fees are set separately. Share your site and idea with Permits Hub and the team can discuss what is involved.'),
    ('faqs', 'Working with Permits Hub', 'Why is having a single point of coordination valuable?', 'Without a coordinator, you become the switchboard managing communications between council planners, architects, traffic engineers, acoustic specialists, surveyors, and builders. Permits Hub provides one clear line of accountability and keeps the entire project team aligned.'),
    ('faqs', 'Working with Permits Hub', 'Which areas in Victoria does Permits Hub service?', 'We work across regional Victoria and metropolitan Melbourne, with publicly documented approvals in Greater Geelong, Ballarat, Melton, and the Surf Coast Shire.'),
    ('services', None, 'Which service should I start with?', 'If you are unsure, start with a conversation. Most projects begin with a feasibility review or advice, and then move to an application when a design is ready.'),
    ('services', None, 'Can Permits Hub guarantee a permit?', 'No planner can guarantee a council decision. The aim is a well-prepared application that addresses the relevant planning controls clearly.'),
    ('services', None, 'What is a request for further information?', 'Council may ask for more detail before it can assess an application. There is usually a deadline to respond, so it helps to act on it promptly.'),
    ('services', None, 'Do I need other consultants?', 'It depends on the site and proposal. Traffic, acoustic, arborist or heritage input is common. The planning review can identify what is likely to be needed.'),
    ('services', None, 'Do you prepare building permits?', 'Permits Hub provides design and documentation to support a building permit application. Building permits themselves are issued by a building surveyor.'),
    ('contact', None, 'Does this form send my enquiry?', 'Yes. When you press send, your enquiry is emailed to the Permits Hub team and a confirmation is sent to your email address. Your details are used only to respond to your enquiry.'),
    ('contact', None, 'Do I need drawings before I get in touch?', 'No. Many conversations start with just an address and an idea. Drawings help later, and early planning input can shape them.'),
    ('contact', None, 'Can I call instead?', 'Yes. Phone 0409 198 597 to talk through your project.'),
    ('contact', None, 'Do you work outside the regions listed on this site?', 'The regions shown are where public updates place Permits Hub’s work. Enquiries from elsewhere in Victoria are welcome; the team can advise whether they can help.'),
    ('contact', None, 'Is the first conversation free?', 'Placeholder for the client to confirm. Fees and scope are agreed before any work begins.'),
]

